---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the RAND-36, drafted for Craig's review.
// FAQ answers come from rand-36.content.ts and are shown word for word.
import { FAQ, REFERENCE, SOURCES } from '../../tools/rand-36.content';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';
const s = SOURCES;
---

<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-rand-36">
	<h2 id="understanding-rand-36">Understanding your RAND-36 scores</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.difference.a}</p>
	<p>{FAQ.scoring.a}</p>
	<p>
		A score of 50 on a scale means your answers averaged halfway between the worst and best possible responses
		(<a href={s.rand.href} rel="noopener">{s.rand.label}</a>).
	</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">Score bands</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.normal.a}</p>
	<table class="interp-table">
		<caption>Average scale scores (0 to 100): Medical Outcomes Study patients and people with ME/CFS</caption>
		<thead>
			<tr><th scope="col">Scale</th><th scope="col">MOS patients (n = 2,471)</th><th scope="col">ME/CFS (n = 170)</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{REFERENCE.map(r => <tr><th scope="row">{r.scale}</th><td>{r.mos}</td><td>{r.mecfs}</td></tr>)}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>{FAQ.mecfs.a}</p>
	<p>
		In that study (<a href={s.nacul2011.href} rel="noopener">{s.nacul2011.label}</a>) physical scores were far lower
		than mental health, the reverse of the pattern in depression. If your profile looks like this, point it out to a
		clinician.
	</p>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.change.a}</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>
		The RAND-36 is a general measure of health-related quality of life. It does not diagnose anything, and it does not
		ask about post-exertional malaise, so it can miss the cost of activity that comes a day or two later. The
		<a href="/tools/dsq-pem/">post-exertional malaise questionnaire</a> and <a href="/tools/funcap/">FUNCAP</a> cover
		that.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>
		Bring all eight scale scores with the date, your answer to the health change question, and your saved results over
		time. Say it is the RAND 36-Item Health Survey, which has the same questions as the SF-36; many clinicians will know it
		by that name. The physical functioning and role
		scales give a concrete picture of what you can no longer do, which can support a referral or a benefits claim
		alongside a clinician's report. It cannot show the cause. See also
		<a href="/nhs/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

	<div class="tool-foot">
		<slot name="disclaimer" />
		<details class="sources" id="sources">
			<summary>Sources and licence</summary>
			<slot name="citation" />
			<ul class="interp-sources">
				<li>Hays RD, Sherbourne CD, Mazel RM. The RAND 36-Item Health Survey 1.0. <em>Health Econ</em> 1993;2(3):217–27. <a href={s.hays1993.href} rel="noopener">Abstract</a></li>
				<li>RAND Corporation. 36-Item Short Form Survey (SF-36) scoring instructions, Tables 1–3. <a href={s.rand.href} rel="noopener">{s.rand.label}</a></li>
				<li>Nacul LC, Lacerda EM, Campion P, et al. The functional status and well being of people with myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome and their carers. <em>BMC Public Health</em> 2011;11:402, Table 2. <a href={s.nacul2011.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
			</ul>
		</details>
	</div>
</section>
